    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d282">

                <pb facs="0443" n="363" xml:id="pg363"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Mutual Guarantee (Locarno)/830:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Phillips</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-10-21T12:00:00+01:00">October 21,
                            1936—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received October 21—9:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>422. My 407, October 9, noon.<anchor n="16a" xml:id="d282-f1"/> The Italian reply
                    to the British proposals with regard to a meeting of the Locarno Powers has been
                    handed to the British Ambassador. Its contents have not been made public. In
                    reply to my inquiry of my British colleague Drummond told me that it was couched
                    in friendly language but expressed Italy’s desire for a return to the original
                    Locarno undertaking rather than the adoption of two agreements of mutual
                    guarantees as proposed by his Government. Drummond was of the opinion that the
                    Italian reply would be a disappointment to London.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
